{"reasoning":"To implement the 367-day inactivity data purge effectively, the ingestion pipeline must incorporate logic to identify and exclude or flag data exceeding this inactivity threshold. Ensuring data quality at ingestion includes validating timestamps against this policy and annotating data accordingly. This approach maintains compliance and supports downstream processes in enforcing data retention rules.","findings":"Given the data source is the CRM database, ingestion will involve extracting customer usage data, verifying 'last activity' timestamps, and applying filters to exclude data beyond 367 days of inactivity. The schema version v2.1 should be used to validate incoming data structure. Data must be encrypted (AES-256) when stored in the Data Lake (S3). Additionally, RBAC policies must be enforced during ingestion to restrict access. An audit log should record purged data instances for compliance verification.","decision":"Design and implement the ingestion pipeline with the following features: 1) data extraction from CRM with timestamp validation against 367-day inactivity; 2) exclusion or flagging of stale data to comply with CRITICAL CONSTRAINT [DATA-RETN-367D]; 3) schema validation against v2.1; 4) encryption of data at rest using AES-256; 5) enforcement of RBAC policies during ingestion; 6) detailed audit logging of purge actions. After implementation, report ingestion status and compliance adherence to the Data Quality Analyst.","confidence":0.95}

